{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the bitter personal and legal battles that accompanied it. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie transforms what could have been a dry corporate drama into a gripping psychological thriller. the screenplay, penned by aaron sorkin, is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue, layered with wit, tension, and emotional undercurrents that never let up.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but socially awkward prodigy whose genius is matched only by his emotional detachment. his portrayal is chillingly accurate—not as a villain, but as a man who sees relationships as transactions and ambition as the only currency that matters. justin timberlake is equally compelling as sean parker, the charismatic, chaotic co-founder of napster who brings both inspiration and destruction to zuckerberg’s world. timberlake radiates a dangerous charm that makes parker both magnetic and deeply unsettling.\n\nthe supporting cast, including andrew garfield as edward sweeney and jesse plemons as the winklevoss twins, is uniformly excellent. the winklevoss twins are portrayed with such uncanny similarity—using digital effects and a single actor in dual roles—that their shared frustration and sense of betrayal feel tragically human. the film’s pacing is relentless, cutting between courtroom depositions and flashbacks to create a narrative structure that mirrors the fragmented nature of memory and legal truth.\n\ntony burling’s cinematography is cold and clinical, reflecting the sterile world of silicon valley, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting electronic score elevates every scene with a sense of isolation and unease. the music doesn’t just accompany the film—it haunts it.\n\nwhat makes the social network truly remarkable is how it transcends its subject matter. yes, it’s about facebook, but it’s also about the cost of innovation, the erosion of friendship in the face of ambition, and the paradox of connection in a hyper-connected world. the film doesn’t offer easy answers; instead, it leaves viewers with a lingering discomfort: was zuckerberg a thief, a visionary, or simply a boy who never learned how to say sorry?\n\nby the end, the screen fades to black, and the final line—“you don’t get to 500 million friends without making a few enemies”—echoes long after the credits roll. the social network isn’t just a great movie; it’s a cultural artifact, a mirror held up to the digital age, and one of the finest films of the 2010s.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0004889961564913392}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.31129345297813416}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.47485053539276123}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.001073380233719945}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0032333259005099535}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00011812942830147222}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -0.8836938738822937}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.4305012882687151e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.4656167030334473}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.10296709835529327}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00035065223346464336}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.3159199059009552}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.062299427052494e-05}, {"token": " bitter", "logprob": -1.946232557296753}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.44379740953445435}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2731937766075134}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -0.0001267114421352744}, {"token": "